```json
{
  "answer": "
  {
    \"definition\": \"A database index is a **data structure** that improves the speed of data retrieval operations on a database table.\",
    \"purpose\": \"It allows the database management system (DBMS) to find data without scanning the entire table, significantly speeding up queries that filter, sort, or join data.\",
    \"analogy\": \"Think of it like an index in a book—you don’t have to read every page to find a specific topic; you can jump directly to the relevant page using the index.\",
    \"types\": [
      {
        \"name\": \"B-tree (Balanced Tree)\",
        \"use_case\": \"Most common for general-purpose indexing (e.g., primary/foreign keys). Handles dynamic data well.\"
      },
      {
        \"name\": \"Hash Index\",
        \"use_case\": \"Fast for exact-match lookups (e.g., `WHERE id = 5`), but inefficient for range queries (e.g., `WHERE age > 30`).\"
      },
      {
        \"name\": \"Bitmap Index\",
        \"use_case\": \"Efficient for low-cardinality columns (e.g., gender, status) in data warehousing.\"
      },
      {
        \"name\": \"Full-Text Index\",
        \"use_case\": \"Optimizes search operations on text data (e.g., `LIKE '%query%'`).\"
      },
      {
        \"name\": \"Composite Index\",
        \"use_case\": \"Combines multiple columns (e.g., `(last_name, first_name)`) to optimize queries filtering on multiple fields.\"
      }
    ],
    \"tradeoffs\": {
      \"pros\": [
        \"Faster read operations.\",
        \"Reduced I/O overhead.\",
        \"Enhanced query performance for filtered/sorted data.\"
      ],
      \"cons\": [
        \"Slower write operations (INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE) due to index maintenance.\",
        \"Increased storage overhead.\",
        \"Risk of over-indexing, leading to performance degradation.\"
      ]
    },
    \"key_points\": [
      {
        \"point\": \"Indexes are **not** free—use them judiciously.\",
        \"example\": \"Avoid indexing columns with low selectivity (e.g., `gender`).\"
      },
      {
        \"point\": \"Primary keys are **automatically indexed** in most DBMS.\",
        \"example\": \"Tables like `users(id, name)` already have an index on `id`.\"
      },
      {
        \"point\": \"Indexes expire: Rebuild them periodically for optimal performance.\"
      }
    ],
    \"example_query\": {
      \"sql\": \"CREATE INDEX idx_customer_name ON customers(last_name, first_name);\",
      \"effect\": \"Speeds up queries like `SELECT * FROM customers WHERE last_name = 'Smith' ORDER BY first_name;`\"
    }
  }
"
}
```

